  </owl:Ontology>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A reified alignment that connects multiple resources.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">alignment</rdfs:label>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Restriction>
        <owl:minCardinality rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:minCardinality>
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/aligns"/>
      </owl:Restriction>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/ExactAlignment">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An alignment with a diameter of exactly 0.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">exact alignment</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Restriction>
        <owl:hasValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.0</owl:hasValue>
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/diameter"/>
      </owl:Restriction>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/ExactMatch">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A reified n-ary alignment describing a SKOS exactMatch group.

This is a convenience class to allow for migrating existing `skos:exactMatch` collections to skal.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">exact match</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Class>
        <owl:intersectionOf>
          <rdf:Description>
            <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/PermanentAlignment"/>
            <rdf:rest>
              <rdf:Description>
                <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/ExactAlignment"/>
                <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
              </rdf:Description>
            </rdf:rest>
          </rdf:Description>
        </owl:intersectionOf>
      </owl:Class>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
    <owl:hasKey rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/aligns"/>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">Individuals of this type employ the same semantics as `skos:exactMatch` assertions.  That is, a second individual that aligns one of the concepts of the first individual is considered `owl:sameAs` this first individual.  And thereby capturing the transitive closure of all concepts on the subject and object side of a `skos:exactMatch` collection.</skos:note>
  </owl:Class>
  <sh:NodeShape rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/ExactMatchRuleShape">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">SHACL rule to materialise pairwise skos:exactMatch triples from skal:ExactMatch individuals.</rdfs:comment>
    <sh:rule>
      <sh:SPARQLRule>
        <sh:construct>
			CONSTRUCT {
				?x skos:exactMatch ?y .
				?y skos:exactMatch ?x .
			}
			WHERE {
				?a a skal:ExactMatch ;
					skal:aligns ?x , ?y .
				FILTER (?x != ?y)
			}
		</sh:construct>
        <sh:prefixes>
          <rdf:Description>
            <sh:declare>
              <rdf:Description>
                <sh:namespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#</sh:namespace>
                <sh:prefix>skos</sh:prefix>
              </rdf:Description>
            </sh:declare>
            <sh:declare>
              <rdf:Description>
                <sh:namespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://purl.org/skal/</sh:namespace>
                <sh:prefix>skal</sh:prefix>
              </rdf:Description>
            </sh:declare>
          </rdf:Description>
        </sh:prefixes>
      </sh:SPARQLRule>
    </sh:rule>
    <sh:targetClass rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/ExactMatch"/>
  </sh:NodeShape>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/MeasuredAlignment">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An alignment with a measured (or otherwise determined) diameter.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">measured alignment</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Class>
        <owl:intersectionOf>
          <rdf:Description>
            <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
            <rdf:rest>
              <rdf:Description>
                <rdf:first>
                  <owl:Restriction>
                    <owl:minCardinality rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:minCardinality>
                    <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/diameter"/>
                  </owl:Restriction>
                </rdf:first>
                <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
              </rdf:Description>
            </rdf:rest>
          </rdf:Description>
        </owl:intersectionOf>
      </owl:Class>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/Metric">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The metric used to express the diameter of an alignment.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">metric</rdfs:label>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">It is (currently) beyond the scope of skal to define named individuals of this type with rigour.  There are four well-known kinds of n-ary metrics:

1. n-metric:
Given a *metric* d between two concepts (i.e. d(x,x) = 0, d(x,y) = d(y,x), and d(x,y) &lt;= d(x,z)+d(z,y)) an *n-metric* can be defined as D(x_1,...,x_n) := Σ_{i&lt;j} d(x_i,x_j), the following properties hold as per the underlying metric's axioms: D(x,...,x) = 0, D(x_1,...,x_n) = D(x_σ(1),...,x_σ(n)), D(x_1,...,x_n) &lt;= d(x_1,z) + D(z,x_2,...,x_n), and monotonicity: D(x_1,...,x_k,x_k,...) &lt;= D(x_1,...,x_k,x_(k+1),x_(k+1),...).

1. n-ultrametric:
Given an *ultrametric* `u` between two concepts (i.e. u(x,x) = 0, u(x,y) = u(y,x), and u(x,y) &lt;= max{u(x,z),u(z,y)}) an *n-ultrametric* can be defined as U(x_1,...,x_n) := max_{i&lt;j} u(x_i,x_j), the following properties hold as per the underlying ultrametric's axioms: U(x,...,x) = 0, U(x_1,...,x_n) = U(x_σ(1),...,x_σ(n)), U(x_1,...,x_n) &lt;= max{u(x_1,z), U(z,x_2,...,x_n), and monotonicity: U(x_1,...,x_k,x_k,...) &lt;= U(x_1,...,x_k,x_(k+1),x_(k+1),...).

1. MST length:
Given a *metric* d between two concepts, the MST length is defined as MST(X):=min_T Σ_(i,j)\in E(T) d(x_i,x_j), where E(T) is the set of edges of the spanning tree T.  The following properties hold: MST({x,...,x}) = 0, MST({x_1,...,x_n})=MST({x_σ(1),...,x_σ(n)}), MST(X∪Y)&lt;=MST(X∪Z)+MST(Z∪Y), and monotonicity: MST({x_1,...,x_k,x_k,...}) &lt;= MST({x_1,...,x_k,x_(k+1),x_(k+1),...}).

1. Steiner tree length:
Given a *metric* d between two concepts, the Steiner tree length is defined as ST(X):=inf_T Σ_(u,v)\in E(T) d(u,v) where E(T) is the set of edges of a tree whose vertex set contains X and any number of Steiner points.  The following properties hold: ST({x,...,x}) = 0, ST({x_1,...,x_n})=ST({x_σ(1),...,x_σ(n)}), ST(X∪Y)&lt;=ST(X∪Z)+ST(Z∪Y), and monotonicity: ST({x_1,...,x_k,x_k,...}) &lt;= ST({x_1,...,x_k,x_(k+1),x_(k+1),...}).

The first two of which can be traced to the metric distance of individual pairs of points (aka concepts) whereas the latter two are global in nature.  ST even employs points (i.e. concepts) that aren't part of the original set of concepts in its definition.

Between these four the following holds over a fixed underlying (binary) metric d: ST &lt;= MST &lt;= U &lt;= D.</skos:note>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/PermanentAlignment">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An alignment that holds permanently.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">permanent alignment</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/TemporaryAlignment"/>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Class>
        <owl:intersectionOf>
          <rdf:Description>
            <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
            <rdf:rest>
              <rdf:Description>
                <rdf:first>
                  <owl:Class>
                    <owl:complementOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/TemporaryAlignment"/>
                  </owl:Class>
                </rdf:first>
                <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
              </rdf:Description>
            </rdf:rest>
          </rdf:Description>
        </owl:intersectionOf>
      </owl:Class>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
  </owl:Class>
  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/TemporaryAlignment">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An alignment that holds during the specified period of validity.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">temporary alignment</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/PermanentAlignment"/>
    <owl:equivalentClass>
      <owl:Class>
        <owl:intersectionOf>
          <rdf:Description>
            <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
            <rdf:rest>
              <rdf:Description>
                <rdf:first>
                  <owl:Class>
                    <owl:unionOf>
                      <rdf:Description>
                        <rdf:first>
                          <owl:Restriction>
                            <owl:minCardinality rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:minCardinality>
                            <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/tempo/validFrom"/>
                          </owl:Restriction>
                        </rdf:first>
                        <rdf:rest>
                          <rdf:Description>
                            <rdf:first>
                              <owl:Restriction>
                                <owl:minCardinality rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:minCardinality>
                                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/tempo/validTill"/>
                              </owl:Restriction>
                            </rdf:first>
                            <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
                          </rdf:Description>
                        </rdf:rest>
                      </rdf:Description>
                    </owl:unionOf>
                  </owl:Class>
                </rdf:first>
                <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
              </rdf:Description>
            </rdf:rest>
          </rdf:Description>
        </owl:intersectionOf>
      </owl:Class>
    </owl:equivalentClass>
  </owl:Class>
  <sh:NodeShape rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/ZeroDiameterRuleShape">
    <sh:rule>
      <sh:SPARQLRule>
        <sh:construct>
			CONSTRUCT {
				?x owl:sameAs ?y .
			}
			WHERE {
				?x a skal:ExactAlignment ;
					skal:aligns ?p .
				?y a skal:ExactAlignment ;
					skal:aligns ?p .
				FILTER(?x != ?y)

				OPTIONAL {
				?x tempo:validFrom ?xfrom
				}
				OPTIONAL {
				?y tempo:validFrom ?yfrom
				}
				FILTER(!BOUND(?xfrom) &amp;&amp; !BOUND(?yfrom) || ?xfrom = ?yfrom)

				OPTIONAL {
				?x tempo:validTill ?xtill
				}
				OPTIONAL {
				?y tempo:validTill ?ytill
				}
				FILTER(!BOUND(?xtill) &amp;&amp; !BOUND(?ytill) || ?xtill = ?ytill)
			}
		</sh:construct>
        <sh:prefixes>
          <rdf:Description>
            <sh:declare>
              <rdf:Description>
                <sh:namespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#</sh:namespace>
                <sh:prefix>owl</sh:prefix>
              </rdf:Description>
            </sh:declare>
            <sh:declare>
              <rdf:Description>
                <sh:namespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://purl.org/skal/</sh:namespace>
                <sh:prefix>skal</sh:prefix>
              </rdf:Description>
            </sh:declare>
            <sh:declare>
              <rdf:Description>
                <sh:namespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://purl.org/tempo/</sh:namespace>
                <sh:prefix>tempo</sh:prefix>
              </rdf:Description>
            </sh:declare>
          </rdf:Description>
        </sh:prefixes>
      </sh:SPARQLRule>
    </sh:rule>
    <sh:targetClass rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
  </sh:NodeShape>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/aligns">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Links an alignment individual to each concept it aligns.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">aligns</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"/>
    <skos:note>This property is explicitly not an owl:InverseFunctionalProperty because the point of reified alignments is that additional properties (like validity or sources) might be associated with the alignment.</skos:note>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:DatatypeProperty rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/diameter">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Measures the "exactness" of an alignment, optionally qualified by the metric in `skal:metric`.

The lower bound of any metric is 0, which holds iff all concepts are exactly aligned.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">diameter</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:range>
      <owl:Datatype>
        <owl:onDatatype rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal"/>
        <owl:withRestrictions>
          <rdf:Description>
            <rdf:first>
              <rdf:Description>
                <xsd:minInclusive rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.0</xsd:minInclusive>
              </rdf:Description>
            </rdf:first>
            <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
          </rdf:Description>
        </owl:withRestrictions>
      </owl:Datatype>
    </rdfs:range>
  </owl:DatatypeProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="http://purl.org/skal/metric">
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Metric used for the diameter value of the alignment.

Because alignments are inherently n-ary the term metric is to be understood in a more general fashion, e.g. n-metric, or n-ultrametric, MST path length, or Steiner tree length, etc.

Moreover, the lower bound of any metric is 0, which holds iff all concepts are exactly aligned.  That means an alignment with diameter 0 need not explicitly state the metric used, and if it does the metric must be considered interchangeable with any other metric.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Alignment"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">metric</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://purl.org/skal/Metric"/>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
</rdf:RDF>
